[agent:result]     Execute a SQL statement and optionally await its results for a specified time.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     **Use case: small result sets with INLINE + JSON_ARRAY**
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     For flows that generate small and predictable result sets (<= 25 MiB), `INLINE` responses of
[agent:result]     `JSON_ARRAY` result data are typically the simplest way to execute and fetch result data.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     **Use case: large result sets with EXTERNAL_LINKS**
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     Using `EXTERNAL_LINKS` to fetch result data allows you to fetch large result sets efficiently. The
[agent:result]     main differences from using `INLINE` disposition are that the result data is accessed with URLs, and
[agent:result]     that there are 3 supported formats: `JSON_ARRAY`, `ARROW_STREAM` and `CSV` compared to only
[agent:result]     `JSON_ARRAY` with `INLINE`.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     ** URLs**
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     External links point to data stored within your workspace's internal storage, in the form of a URL.
[agent:result]     The URLs are valid for only a short period, <= 15 minutes. Alongside each `external_link` is an
[agent:result]     expiration field indicating the time at which the URL is no longer valid. In `EXTERNAL_LINKS` mode,
[agent:result]     chunks can be resolved and fetched multiple times and in parallel.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     ----
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     ### **Warning: Databricks strongly recommends that you protect the URLs that are returned by the
[agent:result]     `EXTERNAL_LINKS` disposition.**
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     When you use the `EXTERNAL_LINKS` disposition, a short-lived, URL is generated, which can be used to
[agent:result]     download the results directly from . As a short-lived is embedded in this URL, you should protect the
[agent:result]     URL.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     Because URLs are already generated with embedded temporary s, you must not set an `Authorization`
[agent:result]     header in the download requests.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     The `EXTERNAL_LINKS` disposition can be disabled upon request by creating a support case.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     See also [Security best practices].
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     ----
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     StatementResponse contains `statement_id` and `status`; other fields might be absent or present
[agent:result]     depending on context. If the SQL warehouse fails to execute the provided statement, a 200 response is
[agent:result]     returned with `status.state` set to `FAILED` (in contrast to a failure when accepting the request,
[agent:result]     which results in a non-200 response). Details of the error can be found at `status.error` in case of
[agent:result]     execution failures.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     [Security best practices]: https://docs.databricks.com/sql/admin/sql-execution-tutorial.html#security-best-practices
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     :param statement: str
[agent:result]       The SQL statement to execute. The statement can optionally be parameterized, see `parameters`. The
[agent:result]       maximum query text size is 16 MiB.
[agent:result]     :param warehouse_id: str
[agent:result]       Warehouse upon which to execute a statement. See also [What are SQL warehouses?]
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       [What are SQL warehouses?]: https://docs.databricks.com/sql/admin/warehouse-type.html
[agent:result]     :param byte_limit: int (optional)
[agent:result]       Applies the given byte limit to the statement's result size. Byte counts are based on internal data
[agent:result]       representations and might not match the final size in the requested `format`. If the result was
[agent:result]       truncated due to the byte limit, then `truncated` in the response is set to `true`. When using
[agent:result]       `EXTERNAL_LINKS` disposition, a default `byte_limit` of 100 GiB is applied if `byte_limit` is not
[agent:result]       explicitly set.
[agent:result]     :param catalog: str (optional)
[agent:result]       Sets default catalog for statement execution, similar to [`USE CATALOG`] in SQL.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       [`USE CATALOG`]: https://docs.databricks.com/sql/language-manual/sql-ref-syntax-ddl-use-catalog.html
[agent:result]     :param disposition: :class:`Disposition` (optional)
[agent:result]       The fetch disposition provides two modes of fetching results: `INLINE` and `EXTERNAL_LINKS`.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       Statements executed with `INLINE` disposition will return result data inline, in `JSON_ARRAY`
[agent:result]       format, in a series of chunks. If a given statement produces a result set with a size larger than 25
[agent:result]       MiB, that statement execution is aborted, and no result set will be available.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       **NOTE** Byte limits are computed based upon internal representations of the result set data, and
[agent:result]       might not match the sizes visible in JSON responses.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       Statements executed with `EXTERNAL_LINKS` disposition will return result data as external links:
[agent:result]       URLs that point to cloud storage internal to the workspace. Using `EXTERNAL_LINKS` disposition
[agent:result]       allows statements to generate arbitrarily sized result sets for fetching up to 100 GiB. The
[agent:result]       resulting links have two important properties:
